---
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro"
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Coolify deployment"
  path="/docs/self-hosting/coolify"
  description="Run KoAkademy on Coolify, the open-source PaaS, from its Docker Compose file."
>
  <p>
    Coolify is a self-hosted PaaS with a built-in Caddy proxy, automatic TLS,
    managed databases, and scheduled backups. You can run it on your own servers
    or on Coolify's hosted infrastructure. This guide deploys KoAkademy as a
    single Docker Compose resource.
  </p>

  <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A Coolify server with a domain assigned to it</li>
    <li>A stable KoAkademy release tag</li>
    <li>An external S3-compatible bucket for uploads</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Install Coolify with its installer script on a fresh Ubuntu or Debian
    server, or add this server to an existing Coolify instance.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Prepare the Compose source</h2>
  <p>
    Fetch KoAkademy's production Compose topology at the stable tag you intend
    to deploy:
  </p>
  <pre><code>git clone --depth 1 --branch &lt;latest-stable-tag&gt; https://github.com/yukazakiri/koakademy.git
cd koakademy</code></pre>
  <p>
    Adapt <code>compose.production.yaml</code> for Coolify the same way you would
    for any proxy-managed platform:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Keep PostgreSQL data on a named volume.</li>
    <li>
      Do not publish host ports for the application — Coolify's Caddy proxy
      routes to the container's port <code>8000</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Pass credentials as environment variables in the resource; Docker secrets
      and the Swarm installer's secret handling do not apply here.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>TRUSTED_PROXIES=*</code> is acceptable because only Coolify's proxy can
      reach the application (see
      <a href="/docs/self-hosting/configuration">Configuration</a>).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Create a Docker Compose resource</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Choose <strong>New Resource → Docker Compose Empty</strong> and paste your compose
      file, or create a resource from your Git repository and select the
      <strong>Docker Compose</strong> build pack with the compose file location.
    </li>
    <li>
      Coolify parses the environment variables out of the compose file and
      exposes them in the UI — individual cards in Normal view, or a bulk
      <code>.env</code> editor in Developer view. Set <code>APP_URL</code>,
      <code>PORTAL_HOST</code>, <code>ADMIN_HOST</code>, <code>APP_KEY</code>,
      <code>DB_*</code>, <code>REDIS_*</code>, <code>GOTENBERG_URL</code>,
      <code>AWS_*</code> storage credentials, <code>MAIL_*</code>, and
      <code>KOAKADEMY_VERSION</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Assign your domain to the application service in the resource's
      <strong>Domains</strong> tab and set the port to <code>8000</code>.
      Coolify's Caddy proxy issues the TLS certificate automatically.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Verify the application answers: <code
      >curl --fail --silent --show-error https://school.example/up</code
    >.
  </p>
  <div class="doc-callout doc-callout-tip">
    <p class="doc-callout-label">Managed databases instead</p>
    <p>
      Coolify can also run PostgreSQL and Redis as dedicated database resources
      with built-in S3 backups. If you use them, point the application's <code
        >DB_HOST</code
      > and <code>REDIS_HOST</code> at the internal hostname Coolify assigns to each
      resource, and keep the database credentials in the application's environment.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>3. Run migrations and complete setup</h2>
  <p>
    KoAkademy keeps <code>RUN_MIGRATIONS=false</code>. First set a stable
    application key: generate one once with
    <code>php artisan key:generate --show</code> and paste the complete value into
    <code>APP_KEY</code> in the resource's environment. A key written only to the
    container's <code>.env</code> would be lost on the next redeploy.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then open the application container's <strong>Terminal</strong> tab and run:
  </p>
  <pre><code>php artisan migrate --force</code></pre>
  <p>
    Visit <code>https://school.example/setup</code> to create the institution, initial
    academic period, and first administrator, then verify
    <code>/admin</code>. If you define a one-off migration container in the
    compose file, add <code>exclude_from_hc: true</code> to its service so it does
    not affect the resource's health evaluation.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Backups</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      For managed database resources, enable Coolify's scheduled backups to an
      S3-compatible destination.
    </li>
    <li>
      For a PostgreSQL service inside the compose file, schedule a
      <code>pg_dump</code> from the container terminal or back up its named volume.
    </li>
    <li>
      Back up the S3 upload bucket separately, and preserve <code>APP_KEY</code> in
      a secret store.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Upgrades</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Read the release notes and take a database backup.</li>
    <li>
      Pin the new stable image tag (<code>KOAKADEMY_VERSION</code>) and
      redeploy.
    </li>
    <li>Run <code>php artisan migrate --force</code> from the terminal.</li>
    <li>
      Smoke-test the portal, <code>/admin</code>, uploads, a queued job, and a
      PDF export.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The upgrade and rollback rules in
    <a href="/docs/self-hosting/deployment">Deployment</a> apply unchanged.
  </p>

  <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the resource logs and
    <a href="/docs/self-hosting/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a>. Common
    Coolify-specific causes: environment changes applied but not redeployed, a
    domain on the wrong service or port, and <code>APP_URL</code> not matching the
    assigned domain.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
